  "description": "The shoulder girdle, axilla, arm and elbow. At the shoulder the deltoid covers the head of the humerus; with the arm abducted two hollows appear below the acromion, in front of and behind the greater tubercle, and deep to them lie the subacromial bursa, the rotator cuff and the capsule of the shoulder joint. On top of the shoulder, between the clavicle and the spine of the scapula, trapezius and supraspinatus lie over the supraspinous fossa (the apex of the lung lies medially and is not modelled here). In the axilla the axillary artery, with the axillary vein medial to it and the cords of the brachial plexus around it, lies in fat at the centre of the fossa. In the arm the brachial artery and the median nerve run in the medial bicipital groove to the cubital fossa, where the artery lies just medial to the biceps tendon, deep to the bicipital aponeurosis, with the median nerve medial to it; the ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle, where it can be rolled under the finger. The radial nerve winds round the back of the humerus in the spiral groove, pierces the lateral intermuscular septum about a third of the way up the arm and runs down between brachialis and brachioradialis in front of the lateral epicondyle, dividing at the elbow. The cephalic vein runs in the lateral bicipital groove and the deltopectoral groove; the basilic vein on the medial side, and the median cubital vein crosses the front of the elbow. Configurations move the olecranon (distally with the elbow flexed) and bring the axillary bundle nearer the skin with the arm abducted; tissue zones do not move with the configuration. Prone, the anterior arm and the axilla face the couch: the engine grants access to the region as a whole.",
